what we're going to do in this video is  talk about factors and factor pairs now  when we talk about factors these are  really numbers that can be multiplied  together to make some number so for  example if I were to talk about factors  of 6 I could multiply 2 times 3 to get 6  and so we would say that 2 &amp; 3 are  factors of 6 in fact we would also say  that 2 &amp; 3 is a factor pair for 6  because when I multiply those 2 I can  get 6 now to think about all of the  different factor pairs for a number we  could think about it in terms of area  how can we make a rectangle with area 6  well you could do it if it's 2 units by  3 units so it could look something like  this I'll just hand draw it so let's say  it has so let's say our rectangle looks  like this so that's 2 rows and then 3  columns and let's say these all have  equal area it's hand drawn but you can  see that the area here would be 2 times  3 which would be equal to 6 square units  now what if what are other ways to get  to an area of 6 well you could have  something that is one row but then it  has six columns so maybe it looks  something like this  it looks like this so it's one row and  then you have 1 2 3 4 5 I want them all  to be roughly the same size so it's 1  times 6 would be also an area of 6 so  that would be another factor pair we  know that 2 times 3 is equal to 6 and we  know that 1 times 6 is equal to 6 and  these are actually the two factor pairs  4 6 and we can do it with larger numbers  we can think about what about all the  factor pairs for something like 16 pause  this video and see if you could think  about that well I'll set up a little  table here to think about that and so in  this column I'll put the first factor  and in this column I'll put the second  factor  and the way I like to do it is I start  at one and I keep working up up to the  number to try to figure out all of the  factors so let's start with 1 so 1 is  definitely divisible into 16 and as long  as you put a whole number here it's  going to be divisible by 1 and what I  have to multiply by 1 to get to 16 we'll  have to multiply it by 16 so that's a  factor pair right there 1 and 16 now  what about 2 does 2 go into 16 well sure  2 times 8 is 16 so that's another factor  pair 2 times 8 we found another factor  pair  now what about 3 does 3 go evenly into  16  well no 3 times 5 is 15 and 3 times 6 is  18 so 3 doesn't go into 16 so 3 would  not be a factor of 16 what about 4 well  4 times 4 is 16 so that's a factor pair  there 4 &amp; 4 what about 5 well no you 5  times 2 is 10 5 times 3 is 15 times 5  times 4 is 20  5 does not go evenly into 16 same thing  for 6 6 times 2 is 12 6 times 3 is 18  does not go evenly into 16 what about 7  7 does not evenly go into 16 7 times 2  is 14 7 times 3 is 21  what about 8 well we already know that 8  goes evenly into 16 you might be tend to  say oh there's another factor pair of 8  times 2 but we already wrote that down  we just happened to say that 2 is the  first factor and 8 is the second factor  but you could say it the other way  around so we don't have to then go to 8  times 2 and once you've gone half way  you could be confident that you've  already found all the factor pairs  because then you could go to 9 9 doesn't  go into it 10 11 12 13 14 15 but then 16  of course is divisible by 16  but we've already written that here in  this factor pair so we have these three  factor pairs 1 in 16 2 &amp; 8 &amp; 4 &amp; 4
 

